# Every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs and the deploy
# provides exactly those. `env_file: .env` fan-out — which hands every container the whole
# estate's secrets — is banned, and this file is what makes the alternative possible: the deploy
# manifest is derived from it.
#
# `src/env.ts` is the source of truth and this file is checked against it in CI. If you add a
# variable there, add it here.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local work. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way.

# --- required --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# This service's own database. It reads no other, and CI fails on a second connection string.
POOL_DATABASE_URL=postgres://pool:pool@127.0.0.1:5432/pool

# Which chains to mine ON THEIR OWN, comma separated. `btc` and `ltc` are the two implemented.
#
# `doge` IS STILL REFUSED BY NAME HERE, and the refusal now says where it belongs instead. Dogecoin
# is merge-mined with Litecoin under AuxPoW: its blocks are won by a Litecoin header carrying a
# commitment to them, so there is no getblocktemplate to poll for it, no coinbase of its own to
# build and no stratum port to serve it on. This pool DOES mine it — as an auxiliary chain, through
# POOL_LTC_AUX_CHAINS below. Adding it here would ask for a listener that cannot exist.
POOL_CHAINS=btc

# The pool's fee, in basis points (100 = 1%). REQUIRED, WITH NO DEFAULT ANYWHERE.
#
# §7.1 of docs/ecosystem/36-multi-chain-and-mining-pool.md records that the fee has not been chosen.
# A default of 0 would be choosing "free" and a default of 200 would be choosing "2%", and either
# one would answer an open product question by omission. Somebody has to type the number. The value
# below is an example for local work and is not a decision.
POOL_FEE_BASIS_POINTS=100

# --- required, once per chain in POOL_CHAINS ---------------------------------------------------
#
# The node URL CARRIES THE RPC CREDENTIAL as HTTP Basic userinfo, which is how bitcoind and its
# forks authenticate and the only way they do. It is never logged, never put in an error and never
# stored — src/rpc.ts and src/env.ts both hold that line, and rpc.test.ts sweeps every failure path
# to prove it. Treat this variable as a secret in the deploy.
#
# There is exactly ONE endpoint per chain, on purpose. getblocktemplate is not a read of settled
# history; it is a request for work a specific node assembled from a specific mempool, and
# submitblock must go back to the same node. Failing over between two nodes mid-job hands miners a
# merkle branch belonging to a block the other node never proposed.

POOL_BTC_NODE_URL=http://rpcuser:rpcpassword@127.0.0.1:8332/
# Where this pool's share of every block it finds is paid. Validated against the node at boot with
# `validateaddress`, and the resulting scriptPubKey is what goes into the coinbase — the address is
# never parsed in this repository, because a pool that decodes bech32 itself and gets it wrong pays
# every block it ever finds to an unspendable output.
POOL_BTC_PAYOUT_ADDRESS=bc1qexampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexam

# POOL_LTC_NODE_URL=http://rpcuser:rpcpassword@127.0.0.1:9332/
# POOL_LTC_PAYOUT_ADDRESS=ltc1qexampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexam

# --- optional: merge-mining, per parent chain (micro-org#29) ------------------------------------
#
# UNSET MEANS THIS POOL MERGE-MINES NOTHING, which is a complete configuration and the one the
# estate runs on 2026-08-09. Set it and every Litecoin job this pool hands out is simultaneously
# work on the aux chain, at no extra hashing: the miner is unchanged, the share is unchanged, and a
# share that happens to meet Dogecoin's target becomes a Dogecoin block as well as a Litecoin share.
#
# Comma separated, and the parent in the variable name is checked against AUX_PARENT in
# src/chains.ts rather than taken on trust. `doge` may only be merged into `ltc`. Dogecoin's own
# consensus is looser than that — it accepts a parent from any chain — and this pool is tighter on
# purpose: a Dogecoin block is worth what it is worth because its parent is Litecoin and the two
# share scrypt. Merging doge into Bitcoin's SHA-256d work is not forbidden by Dogecoin and produces
# no aux blocks and no errors, which is the exact failure this check exists to make loud.
#
# ALL THREE VARIABLES ARE ALL-OR-NOTHING. Naming an aux chain without its node URL, or without its
# payout address, is refused at boot; so is configuring a node for a chain nobody merges. Every one
# of those half-configurations produces a pool that mines Litecoin perfectly and mines no Dogecoin
# at all, and the only symptom is an absence nobody notices for as long as no aux block would have
# been won anyway.
# POOL_LTC_AUX_CHAINS=doge

# The aux chain's own node, and its own payout address ON ITS OWN CHAIN — a Dogecoin address, not
# the Litecoin one above. The node builds the aux block and is paid by it; this pool never builds a
# Dogecoin coinbase and never chooses that reward's destination, dogecoind does, from this address.
#
# The credential warning above applies unchanged: this URL carries HTTP Basic userinfo and is a
# secret in the deploy.
# POOL_DOGE_NODE_URL=http://rpcuser:rpcpassword@127.0.0.1:22555/
# POOL_DOGE_PAYOUT_ADDRESS=DExampleExampleExampleExampleExampl

# --- optional, with the defaults shown ---------------------------------------------------------

# The HTTP port. The stratum listeners are separate ports, one per chain, below.
PORT=4146
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
POOL_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# mainnet or testnet. Checked against what each node reports from `getblockchaininfo` at boot: a
# mainnet-configured pool pointed at a testnet node refuses to start rather than mine worthless
# blocks with real hardware.
POOL_NETWORK=mainnet

# The stratum listeners. One port per chain, defaulted apart so two chains on one host cannot
# collide by accident. Plain TCP and unauthenticated — that is Stratum v1 as deployed hardware
# speaks it, and it is why these are not the HTTP port.
# POOL_BTC_STRATUM_PORT=3333
# POOL_LTC_STRATUM_PORT=3334

# Which interface the stratum listeners bind. Every interface by default, because that is what a
# compose network needs; narrow it if the pool is exposed directly.
# POOL_STRATUM_BIND=0.0.0.0

# THE ENDPOINT A MINER DIALS. Unset by default, and the default means "nothing is published".
#
# These two are the only place this service learns what a stranger types into their firmware, and
# there is no way for it to work the rest out. The bind above is an interface, not a name; the
# hostname serving the console is a different protocol on a different port and, on this estate,
# provably not reachable for stratum — the console arrives through a Cloudflare Tunnel and Traefik
# and neither carries raw TCP. So `/v1/pool` reports `stratumEndpoint: null` until both halves are
# set, and micro-pool-web says an operator has not published one. That is the correct screen for a
# pool on a LAN or on loopback, which is every deployment on 2026-08-09.
#
# Set them ONLY once the port is genuinely reachable at that name. A connection string that looks
# right and does not connect costs a stranger a silent outage they will blame on their hardware,
# which is worse than being told to ask.
#
# The port is per chain and is NOT defaulted from the bound port, because a deploy maps the two and
# they diverge. This estate's compose file publishes `${POOL_LTC_STRATUM_PORT:-3334}:3334` — the
# host side reads a variable, the container side is a literal — so an operator who moves the
# published port must say so here as well.
#
# Half of a pair is refused at boot: a host with no published port advertises nothing, and a port
# with no host is not an endpoint.
# POOL_STRATUM_PUBLIC_HOST=stratum.example.com
# POOL_BTC_STRATUM_PUBLIC_PORT=3333
# POOL_LTC_STRATUM_PUBLIC_PORT=3334

# --- optional: browser mining (micro-org#289) --------------------------------------------------
#
# Three variables, all optional and all unset by default. Unset means this pool serves mining
# hardware over raw TCP and nothing else, which is a complete, supported and tested configuration:
# a pool run by somebody with no estate at all must start, so a JWKS is never mandatory here.
#
# Set all three and the pool grows a second transport — Stratum v1 over WebSocket, on the HTTP port
# above at /v1/pool/stratum/<chain> — that a browser tab can reach. Raw TCP is untouched by it: a
# miner on the stratum port still authorises with a free-text username, has no estate account, and
# gets the hardware difficulty band, exactly as before.
#
# Verifying an estate access token needs both halves and half of the pair is refused at boot. A key
# set with no issuer verifies a signature and then accepts a token minted by anybody who can serve
# those keys; an issuer with no key set verifies nothing at all.
# IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://identity:4101/.well-known/jwks.json
# IDENTITY_ISSUER=https://identity.example.com

# THE ORIGIN A BROWSER DIALS. Unset means "nothing is published", exactly as above.
#
# One absolute origin, scheme included, because that is what `new WebSocket(...)` takes and because
# a page served over https may only open wss: while a developer on localhost needs ws:. This service
# appends its own path and the chain, so a path typed here is refused at boot — it would be a second
# copy of a contract this repository moves, and the two would diverge invisibly.
#
# Reported on GET /v1/pool as `websocketEndpoint`, per chain, complete. Null until this is set: the
# service cannot observe the name it is reached at, and inventing one from the request Host is the
# defect micro-org#285 records. Setting this without IDENTITY_JWKS_URL is refused, because it would
# advertise an address at which every upgrade is refused.
# POOL_WEBSOCKET_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=wss://pool.example.com

# --- optional: payouts (micro-org#302) ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# ALL UNSET BY DEFAULT, AND UNSET MEANS PAYOUTS ARE OFF. Off is a complete, supported and tested
# configuration, and it is the one every deployment runs in on 2026-08-09: blocks are found,
# recorded, re-checked for maturity and reported, and nothing credits anybody.
#
# Concretely, unset means no sink is constructed and NEITHER payout job is scheduled: not
# `pool.credit-blocks`, which walks matured blocks into per-worker claims, and not
# `pool.flush-payouts`, which posts a claim whose ledger response was lost. Setting a minimum for a
# chain switches both on for that chain and nothing on for any other.
#
# They are optional rather than required — unlike POOL_FEE_BASIS_POINTS above, which is required
# for exactly the reason a minimum payout would be — because src/env.ts is eager and exits the
# process on a refusal. A newly required variable is a container that will not boot, and the estate's
# pool is running right now with none of these set: adding one as required would fail the next
# deploy at boot and take its `--wait` down with it.
#
# SETTING THEM IS STILL NOT ENOUGH TO PAY ANYBODY. A second gate, CUSTODY_BACKING_CLOSED in
# src/payouts.ts, refuses every credit in this release: the pool's payout address is not registered
# with the indexer and is not matched by INDEXER_CUSTODY_LABEL_PREFIXES, so a credit would raise the
# ledger's custody total above what the reconciliation sweep can observe and freeze LTC withdrawals
# estate-wide. That gate is a constant in the source, not a variable, so opening it is a code change
# somebody reviews. Read its comment before setting anything here.
#
# The minimum a miner must be owed before an entry is posted, PER CHAIN, in the smallest unit —
# litoshi for LTC, satoshi for BTC. NO DEFAULT, and a malformed value is refused rather than
# rounded: digits only, so "0.05" is an error and not zero. Setting one for a chain that is not in
# POOL_CHAINS is refused too, because it would pay nobody while looking configured.
# POOL_BTC_MINIMUM_PAYOUT=5000000
# POOL_LTC_MINIMUM_PAYOUT=5000000

# A merge-mined chain is paid on exactly the same terms, and its minimum is INDEPENDENT of its
# parent's. They are different assets, in different smallest units, at prices four orders of
# magnitude apart: 5,000,000 litoshi is about a twentieth of an LTC and 5,000,000 koinu is about
# five cents' worth of DOGE. Deriving one from the other would be arithmetic on an exchange rate
# this service does not have and must not guess. Setting this for a chain no parent merges is
# refused, for the same reason as above — it would look configured and pay nobody.
# POOL_DOGE_MINIMUM_PAYOUT=100000000

# The ledger, which owns every balance in the platform — this service holds none and a miner's
# balance is the ledger's answer, never a number kept here. Required as soon as any minimum above
# is set, and refused if set without one.
# LEDGER_URL=http://ledger:4000

# THIS SERVICE'S OWN SERVICE CREDENTIAL. Treat as a secret. Long-lived, minted by identity with a
# `cfsc_` prefix, and exchanged at boot for the short-lived tokens the ledger accepts. A JWT pasted
# here is refused at boot rather than an hour later, which is micro-org#197/#222.
#
# As of 2026-08-09 the `pool` service holds no service-token grants at all, so one would have to be
# issued with `ledger:post` before this could work.
# POOL_IDENTITY_CREDENTIAL=

# Where the credential is exchanged. Defaults to IDENTITY_ISSUER above, which is the live path on
# this estate — no container sets IDENTITY_URL, and the issuer is identity's own base URL. Set it
# only if the two genuinely differ.
# IDENTITY_URL=http://identity:4000

# How long a single call to the ledger may take. A timeout, not a policy, so unlike the minimum it
# has a default.
# POOL_LEDGER_DEADLINE_MS=8000

# Where a connection's difficulty starts before vardiff has measured anything. Two orders of
# magnitude apart because the hardware is: a SHA-256d ASIC does terahashes, a scrypt miner is
# thousands of times slower in share terms. Vardiff moves off these within a window or two.
# POOL_BTC_INITIAL_DIFFICULTY=65536
# POOL_LTC_INITIAL_DIFFICULTY=512

# The shares-per-minute vardiff steers each connection toward.
# POOL_VARDIFF_SHARES_PER_MINUTE=12

# Bytes written into every coinbase this pool builds, identifying it on the chain. Capped at 32
# bytes: the scriptSig it lives in is capped at 100 by consensus and shares that space with the
# BIP34 height and the extranonce, so an over-long tag does not truncate — it makes the coinbase
# invalid. Refused at boot rather than at the first block.
# POOL_COINBASE_TAG=/cloudsforge/

# The PPLNS window, as a multiple of network difficulty. 2 means the last 2 × D difficulty-weighted
# shares are paid when a block is found.
# POOL_PPLNS_WINDOW_MULTIPLIER=2

# How long a share stays on file after it has fallen out of every window. Shares are a debt record,
# so this is a retention policy and not a cache eviction.
# POOL_SHARE_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# How often getblocktemplate is polled. The node's longpollid is used when it offers one, so this is
# the ceiling on template staleness rather than the normal path.
# POOL_TEMPLATE_POLL_MS=10000

# Names this replica in jobs.locked_by. Defaults to the hostname, which is the container id under
# compose and the pod name under Kubernetes.
# INSTANCE_ID=

# The image tag, reported on every log line and in the release manifest.
# CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev

# --- not read by this service ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There is deliberately NO OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET here, and the absence is load-bearing rather than
# an oversight. Nothing in this release publishes an event, so there is nothing to sign. The mining
# ticket added by micro-org#289 is a random opaque value matched by equality and not a MAC, so it
# needs no key either, and POOL_IDENTITY_CREDENTIAL above is presented rather than signed with.
#
# Every route that answers a question about work already done is still public, because the only
# identity a miner has is the stratum username they chose — gating a miner's own share history
# behind an estate login would exclude every miner who does not have an estate account, which is
# nearly all of them. IDENTITY_* above gates exactly one route, POST /v1/pool/ticket.
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and the rest of OTEL_* are read by the OpenTelemetry SDK loaded ahead
# of the process, not by src/env.ts. They belong in the deploy, not here.
#
# POOL_TEST_DATABASE_URL is read only by the test suite, and only when its name contains "test" —
# the database-backed tests truncate.
